+// A 64-tile-wide background is two 32x32 screen blocks side by side, not one
+// 64-wide array: a tile with x below 32 is in the first block, the rest are
+// 0x400 entries further on, and a row is still 32 wide inside each. Writing it
+// as though it were linear scatters every tile between the two, which draws a
+// forest of duplicated columns with the player nowhere in it.
+// The buffer holds twice what the hardware's screen shows, and a centred
+// viewport reaches equally far both ways -- so it has to start left of the old
+// screen edge, not at it. Half the extra, in metatiles: the camera fills 32
+// where the screen shows 15, and the eight either side are what a wider window
+// sees. Without this the right half is drawn and the left half is whatever was
+// there before, until walking west happens to redraw it.
+#define VIEW_MARGIN 8
+
+static u32 TilemapOffset(u32 x, u32 y)
+{
+    return (x >= 32 ? 0x400 : 0) + y * 32 + (x & 31);
+}
